    },
    "statement": "The actual determinant-conditioned Gaussian mixtures have nonnegative measurable Lebesgue-integrable densities of mass one. P_b(dx)=q_b(x)\\,dx,\\qquad q_b\\ge0,\\quad q_b\\in L^1(dx),\\quad\\int q_b(x)\\,dx=1,\\qquad b\\in\\{-1,+1\\}. Exactly \\(n\\in\\mathbb N,\\ n\\ge3\\) and \\(\\kappa\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\kappa\\ge4\\), including both endpoints and odd dimensions. Every \\(n/2\\) is a real exponent. \\(\\mu\\) is the canonical full-orthogonal Haar probability and \\(\\nu_b=2\\mu\\!\\restriction\\{\\det O=b\\}\\). The actual mixture is \\(P_b=\\nu_b\\mathrm{bind}\\gamma\\), where \\(\\gamma_O=\\mathcal N(0,A_O^{-1})\\). The source density \\(p_b\\) is determined only Lebesgue-a.e. We choose the literal average \\(q_b\\) below and certify it by equality of actual measures. No pointwise equality to a different arbitrary source representative is asserted. Pointwise inner absolute integrability, measurability, matrix positivity, actual mixture probability, outer L1, normalization and the measure identity are proved inside the theorem. None is a caller hypothesis; totalized Bochner integration supplies no fallback value. A_O=\\frac{\\kappa+1}{2}I-\\frac{\\kappa-1}{4}(O+O^\\top),\\quad\\gamma_O=\\mathcal N(0,A_O^{-1}),\\quad p_O(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{\\det A_O}}{(2\\pi)^{n/2}}\\exp\\!\\left(-\\frac{x^\\top A_Ox}{2}\\right). \\nu_b=2\\mu\\!\\restriction\\{\\det O=b\\},\\quad P_b=\\nu_b\\mathrm{bind}\\gamma,\\quad q_b(x)=\\int p_O(x)\\,d\\nu_b(O).",
